 \begin{abstract}
-Model metadata and its associated resources participate in executable interfaces through both generated bindings and runtime decoding. We study these mechanisms while holding model computation fixed. In an EfficientNet classifier, deleting one associated label preserves the graph, weights, embedded metadata, generated Java, compiled code, and tested numerical outputs, but changes an actual generated Android getter from success to an exception after successful inference. Restoring the labels restores success. A predetermined, count-preserving label permutation instead completes every tested stage while changing 999 of 1,000 exposed label strings; scores and output positions remain unchanged. An official Task classifier rejects the identical deletion artifact during initialization, establishing different enforcement stages for the two configured consumers. A secondary Hand case supplies a source-backed output-role mismatch, yet its metadata exchange leaves the tested official Task result unchanged. We distinguish structural acceptance, reference-backed association agreement, and selected-decoder compatibility, with intervention preservation checked separately. A frozen-rule application to 15 new graph groups and a scoped pre-flight procedure document abstentions and unsupported paths. These controlled results establish interface consequences and consumer boundaries, not defect prevalence, prediction accuracy, or a general semantic validator.
